CAT = "iiqeLaw"; SEG = "LAW"
Q = [
("The simplest legal definition of a contract is:",
 "A legally enforceable agreement", ["Any promise between two people", "An agreement made in writing", "A document signed under seal"],
 "Not every agreement is a contract; a lunch appointment is an agreement with no legal consequences. A contract is an agreement the law will enforce."),
("An insured's policy document is destroyed in the same fire she is claiming for. What is the effect on her cover?",
 "The contract still exists", ["The contract ends with the policy", "The insurer may refuse the claim", "A new proposal must be made"],
 "A contract is intangible; the policy is only evidence of it. Losing the document does not end the insurance contract."),
("A 'simple contract' is one that is:",
 "Formed without formalities", ["Always about simple matters", "Valid only if in writing", "Signed, sealed and delivered"],
 "Simple contracts may be oral, written (not under seal) or inferred from conduct; 'simple' refers to how easily they are formed, not their subject."),
("Most insurance contracts are classed as:",
 "Simple contracts", ["Contracts by deed", "Unilateral promises", "Void agreements"],
 "The great majority of insurance contracts are simple contracts. They are almost always evidenced in writing in practice, though writing is not generally required."),
("A suretyship bond is normally issued as a deed because:",
 "The obligee may give no consideration", ["Deeds are cheaper to prepare", "Simple contracts cannot be put in writing at all", "The law bans oral guarantees"],
 "A promise in a deed is enforceable without consideration, so the surety cannot defend a claim by saying the obligee gave nothing in return."),
("A contract that has no legal effect at all from the start is described as:",
 "Void", ["Voidable", "Unenforceable", "Conditional"],
 "A void contract does not exist in law. In insurance, premiums paid under a void agreement are generally returnable, and so are claims paid."),
("A policy that remains valid unless the insurer chooses to avoid it after finding a material misstatement is:",
 "Voidable", ["Void", "Unenforceable", "Illegal"],
 "A voidable contract is effective unless and until the aggrieved party elects to treat it as void, within a reasonable time of learning of the right."),
("A contract that cannot be sued on until a required step, such as issuing a marine policy, is taken is:",
 "Unenforceable", ["Void", "Voidable", "Illegal from the start"],
 "An unenforceable contract is not void; it simply cannot be enforced until the missing step is taken, which can even be done after a loss."),
("An insurer replies to a proposal by asking for a higher premium than the proposer offered. In law this is:",
 "A counter-offer", ["An acceptance", "A consideration", "A ratification"],
 "Varying the terms of an offer is a counter-offer. It cancels the original offer and must itself be accepted by the proposer."),
("In an insurance contract, the insurer's consideration is:",
 "Its promise to pay claims", ["The claim money actually paid", "The policy document issued", "The commission it pays agents"],
 "Consideration is the promise, not the payment. An insurer that pays no claim during the year has still given consideration, so no premium is returnable."),
("An insured event happens before the promised premium is paid, and the policy does not require payment first. Generally:",
 "He can claim but still owes premium", ["The contract is void for lack of payment", "The insurer pays half the claim", "The policy becomes voidable"],
 "The promise to pay premium is the insured's consideration. The insured is covered, and the insurer has a separate claim for the unpaid premium."),
("A contract made by a minor is generally:",
 "Voidable at the minor's option", ["Void from the start", "Fully binding on both sides", "Unenforceable by the minor"],
 "Lack of capacity through minority or mental disorder generally makes a contract voidable at the option of the person lacking capacity."),
("Insurance arranged on goods known to be smuggled fails which element of a valid contract?",
 "Legality", ["Capacity", "Acceptance", "Consideration"],
 "The subject of a contract must be legal. Insurance on smuggled goods is not legally recognised."),
("Why is intention to create legal relations rarely a problem with insurance contracts?",
 "Business deals are presumed to intend it", ["Insurance law does not require it", "Policies are always signed as deeds", "Insurers state it on every receipt"],
 "Unlike social or domestic arrangements, commercial agreements are presumed to be made with the intention that they have legal consequences."),
("Under the common law doctrine of privity of contract, a third party generally:",
 "Cannot enforce the contract", ["Must perform the contract", "Can always sue for any benefit", "Becomes a party on request"],
 "Privity means only the parties can enforce or be bound by a contract. The Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Ordinance (Cap. 623) creates exceptions."),
("Under Cap. 623, a third party named in a contract may enforce a term that:",
 "Purports to benefit him", ["He has paid consideration for only", "Was agreed before he was born, only", "Imposes a duty on him"],
 "Cap. 623 lets an identified third party enforce a term if the contract says so or the term purports to benefit him, unless the contract shows otherwise."),
("The principle at the root of the law of agency is that:",
 "Acting through another is acting oneself", ["An agent is always paid commission by the principal", "An agent must be licensed", "A principal is never bound by agents"],
 "The principal is bound by the authorised, and sometimes unauthorised, acts of the agent, as if he had done them himself."),
("A mother is bound to pay for groceries her son bought on credit at her request. Her liability arises from:",
 "The law of agency", ["The law of tort", "Subrogation", "Privity of contract"],
 "The son acted as his mother's agent, so the contract of sale is between the shop and the mother, who must pay."),
("Can an agency relationship exist without a contract between principal and agent?",
 "Yes, it can arise without one", ["No, a written contract is needed", "Only if a fee is paid", "Only for licensed agents"],
 "Agency is a relationship. A child buying sugar for a parent is a gratuitous agent with authority, though domestic arrangements are not contracts."),
("A principal later confirms an act his agent had no authority to do at the time. This is:",
 "Ratification", ["Revocation", "Estoppel", "Delegation of authority"],
 "Ratification gives retrospective authority, effectively backdating approval, and can be done in writing, orally or by conduct."),
("An agent authorised only for household insurance grants fire cover; the insurer later accepts it. The fire contract is:",
 "Valid from the start", ["Void and cannot be cured", "Valid from acceptance onwards only", "Voidable by the insured only"],
 "The cover was given without authority, but the insurer's later confirmation ratifies it, so the contract becomes valid retrospectively."),
("Authority that the principal deliberately gives the agent, orally or in writing, is:",
 "Express actual authority", ["Apparent authority", "Implied actual authority", "Authority of necessity"],
 "Actual authority comes from the principal's consent given to the agent; express actual authority is deliberately given in words."),
("An agent forbidden to accept certain risks has often done so, with the principal issuing policies each time. The principal may be bound by:",
 "Apparent authority", ["Express authority", "Ratification only", "Authority of necessity"],
 "Past dealings show third parties that the agent has authority. Apparent authority binds the principal to the third party despite the private restriction."),
("A neighbour pays a hospitalised friend's household renewal premium to keep cover in force. The neighbour is most likely:",
 "An agent of necessity", ["An agent by estoppel", "A secondary party", "An apparent agent"],
 "In an emergency with no way to contact the owner, a person who acts to protect the owner's interests becomes an agent of necessity and can be reimbursed."),
("The doctrine of agency by estoppel applies where:",
 "A non-agent is allowed to appear as one", ["An agent exceeded wide authority", "The principal is living overseas", "The agency has been ratified"],
 "Estoppel stops a person denying an 'agent' he let appear authorised. Apparent authority instead concerns a real agent who seems to have more authority than given."),
("An agent hands his duties to another person without authority to do so. Which duty is breached?",
 "Personal performance", ["Accountability", "Obedience", "Due care and skill"],
 "An agent must not delegate authority and responsibilities to sub-agents unless authorised to do so."),
("The standard of care an agent owes the principal is:",
 "Reasonable skill and diligence", ["Perfection in every task", "No duty unless paid", "Whatever the agent thinks is fit"],
 "The law does not demand perfection; the agent must show reasonably expected skill and diligence, and may have to repay losses caused by lack of care."),
("An agent's duty to keep records and hand over all money received for the principal is the duty of:",
 "Accountability", ["Obedience", "Personal performance", "Due care"],
 "The agent must account for all money or property received on the principal's behalf and keep adequate records of the agency."),
("Which is a duty owed by the principal to the agent?",
 "Reimbursing proper expenses", ["Following all agent instructions", "Keeping the agent's accounts", "Delegating to sub-agents"],
 "The principal must pay agreed remuneration and reimburse costs properly and reasonably incurred by the agent on the principal's behalf."),
("An agency agreement for a fixed two-year period comes to an end on expiry because of:",
 "Time", ["Revocation", "Breach", "Illegality"],
 "Where the agreement is for a set period, it ends when that period runs out, without any action by either side."),
("The death of the principal affects an agency agreement by:",
 "Ending it", ["Passing it to the agent", "Suspending it for a year", "Converting it to a deed"],
 "Agency is a personal relationship, so the death of either party ends it; liquidation has the same effect for a company."),
("An exclusive agent finds the principal has appointed a second agent in breach of contract. The exclusive agent may:",
 "End the agreement and sue", ["Only continue until expiry", "Ratify the second appointment", "Keep acting with no remedy"],
 "A fundamental breach lets the innocent party treat the contract as terminated and claim compensation, such as profit lost for the remaining period."),
]
